# ReservationApp Backend - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
- **Language**: C# (.NET 8)
- **Framework**: ASP.NET Core Web API
- **Database**: PostgreSQL
- **Architecture**: Clean Architecture + Onion Principles

## Coding Conventions

### Coding Style
- Follow Microsoft C# conventions (PascalCase for classes, camelCase for locals, constants in UPPER_CASE)
- Use nullable reference types enabled
- Use expression-bodied members when appropriate
- Avoid public setters on entities — prefer methods for state change

### Error Handling
- Use middleware for exception handling
- No try/catch blocks in controllers unless domain-specific
- Handle errors via centralized middleware returning ProblemDetails JSON

### Async/Await
- Use async/await for all I/O operations

### Logging
- Use Serilog with structured logs

### Validation
- Use FluentValidation for DTO validation

### Dependency Injection
- Register dependencies via interfaces only
- No direct `new` keyword in controllers or services

## Project Structure

### Solution: ReservationApp.sln
- **ReservationApp.API**: Entry point (Controllers, Dependency Injection, Swagger, Middleware, Startup)
- **ReservationApp.Application**: Business logic layer (Services, Interfaces, DTOs, Validators, Commands/Queries)
- **ReservationApp.Domain**: Core domain (Entities, ValueObjects, Enums, Interfaces)
- **ReservationApp.Infrastructure**: Persistence + External services (DbContext, EF Core, Repositories, Configs)
- **ReservationApp.Tests**: Unit + Integration tests

## Architecture Rules

### Controllers
- Controllers should not contain business logic
- Each controller method should call exactly one service method
- Return IActionResult with typed DTO responses, never domain entities

### Services
- Implement all services in Application layer using interface-first approach
- Keep them stateless where possible
- Throw custom exceptions (e.g. DomainException) for business rule violations

### Domain Layer
- No dependencies on other layers
- Contain only Entities, Enums, Interfaces, and Domain Exceptions
- Entities should use constructor injection for required fields

### Infrastructure Layer
- Use EF Core with Npgsql for PostgreSQL
- DbContext should live here, and only repositories access it
- Configure EF entities in separate TypeConfiguration files
- Use migrations via: `dotnet ef migrations add <Name> -p ReservationApp.Infrastructure -s ReservationApp.API`

### Application Layer
- Map domain entities to DTOs via AutoMapper profiles
- Use MediatR (optional) for CQRS commands/queries if complexity grows
- Business rules live here, not in controllers

## Security

- Use JWT Authentication
- Store secrets in appsettings.Development.json or environment variables, never hardcoded
- Add CORS policy explicitly for React + Mobile origins
- Enforce HTTPS redirect

## Testing

- Write unit tests for Application and Domain layers
- Use Moq or NSubstitute for mocking interfaces
- Keep integration tests isolated using an in-memory PostgreSQL instance

## API Standards

- Use RESTful naming (GET /api/reservations/{id})
- Return 201 on creation with Location header
- Handle errors via centralized middleware returning ProblemDetails JSON

### Example Endpoints
- `GET /api/reservations`: Returns all reservations with pagination
- `POST /api/reservations`: Creates a new reservation with validation
- `GET /api/users/{id}`: Fetch user profile
- `POST /api/auth/login`: Authenticate and return JWT

## Tools & Packages

### NuGet Packages
- Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore
- Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL
- FluentValidation.AspNetCore
- Serilog.AspNetCore
- Swashbuckle.AspNetCore
- xUnit
- Moq

## Quality Checks

Before considering work complete, verify:
- Project builds successfully
- All tests pass
- Swagger docs load correctly
- CORS + Auth configured
- No controller directly calls DbContext

## Git Conventions

- Commit small, atomic changes with descriptive messages
- Never commit credentials or appsettings with secrets
- Branch naming convention: `feature/<name>`, `fix/<name>`, `refactor/<name>`

## Primary Goal

Generate maintainable, layered .NET 8 backend with clean separation of concerns and PostgreSQL persistence.
